(; constants used by Mathlib.Data.Rat.Defs, declared with their translated types ;)

(; doc: The Boolean values, `true` and `false`.  Logically speaking, this is equivalent to `Prop` (the type of propositions). The distinction is public important for programming: both propositions and their proofs are erased in the code generator, while `Bool` corresponds to the Boolean type in most programming languages and carries precisely one bit of run-time information. ;)
(; //LEAN:Bool ;)
Bool : Set.

(; doc: The Boolean value `true`, not to be confused with the proposition `True`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Bool.true ;)
Bool__true : Elem Bool.

(; doc: The equality relation. It has one introduction rule, `Eq.refl`. We use `a = b` as notation for `Eq a b`. A fundamental property of equality is that it is an equivalence relation. ``` variable (α : Type) (a b c d : α) variable (hab : a = b) (hcb : c = b) (hcd : c = d)  example : a = d :=   Eq.trans (Eq.trans hab (Eq.symm hcb)) hcd ``` Equality is much more than an equivalence relation, however. It has the important property that every assertion respects the equivalence, in the sense that we can substitute equal expressions without changing the truth value. That is, given `h1 : a = b` and `h2 : p a`, we can construct a proof for `p b` using substitution: `Eq.subst h1 h2`. Example: ``` example (α : Type) (a b : α) (p : α → Prop)         (h1 : a = b) (h2 : p a) : p b :=   Eq.subst h1 h2  example (α : Type) (a b : α) (p : α → Prop)     (h1 : a = b) (h2 : p a) : p b :=   h1 ▸ h2 ``` The triangle in the second presentation is a macro built on top of `Eq.subst` and `Eq.symm`, and you can enter it by typing `\t`. For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality)  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `=` in identifiers is `eq`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq ;)
Eq : (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; doc: If `h : α = β` is a proof of type equality, then `h.mp : α → β` is the induced "cast" operation, mapping elements of `α` to elements of `β`.  You can prove theorems about the resulting element by induction on `h`, since `rfl.mp` is definitionally the identity function. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.mp ;)
Eq__mp : (h : Proof (Eqt Set X Y)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Elem Y.

(; doc: Equality is transitive: if `a = b` and `b = c` then `a = c`.  Because this is in the `Eq` namespace, if you have variables or expressions `h₁ : a = b` and `h₂ : b = c`, you can use `h₁.trans h₂ : a = c` as shorthand for `Eq.trans h₁ h₂`.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.trans ;)
Eq__trans : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (c : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X a b)) -> (h1 : Proof (Eqt X b c)) -> Proof (Eqt X a c).

(; doc: A function `f : α → β` is called injective if `f x = f y` implies `x = y`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.Injective ;)
Function__Injective : (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:Function.Injective.comp ;)
Function__Injective__comp : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (Z : Set) -> (g : (Elem Y -> Z)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (hg : Proof (Function__Injective g)) -> (hf : Proof (Function__Injective f)) -> Proof (Function__Injective (Function__comp g f)).

(; doc: **Alias** of `not_congr`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Iff.not ;)
Iff__not : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (h : Proof (iff a b)) -> Proof (iff (not a) (not b)).

(; doc: The absolute value of an integer is its distance from `0`.  This function is overridden by the compiler with an efficient implementation. This definition is the logical model.  Examples:  * `(7 : Int).natAbs = 7`  * `(0 : Int).natAbs = 0`  * `(-11 : Int).natAbs = 11` ;)
(; //LEAN:Int.natAbs ;)
Int__natAbs : (m : Elem Int) -> Elem Nat.

(; //LEAN:Int.natCast_inj ;)
Int__natCast_inj : (m : Elem Nat) -> (n : Elem Nat) -> Proof (iff (Eq m n) (Eq m n)).

(; doc: `a⁻¹` computes the inverse of `a`. The meaning of this notation is type-dependent.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `⁻¹` in identifiers is `inv`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Inv.inv ;)
Inv__inv : (x : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: **Alias** of `ne_of_gt`. ;)
(; //LEAN:LT.lt.ne' ;)
LT__lt__ne' : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Preorder X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Lt b a)) -> Proof (Neq a b).

(; doc: `m` and `n` are coprime, or relatively prime, if their `gcd` is 1. ;)
(; //LEAN:Nat.Coprime ;)
Nat__Coprime : (m : Elem Nat) -> (n : Elem Nat) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:Nat.mul_ne_zero ;)
Nat__mul_ne_zero : (n : Elem Nat) -> (m : Elem Nat) -> (h : Proof (Neq n 0)) -> (h1 : Proof (Neq m 0)) -> Proof (Neq (times n m) 0).

(; //LEAN:Nat.pos_of_ne_zero ;)
Nat__pos_of_ne_zero : (n : Elem Nat) -> (h : Proof (Neq n 0)) -> Proof (Lt 0 n).

(; doc: `-a` computes the negative or opposite of `a`. The meaning of this notation is type-dependent.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `-` in identifiers is `neg` (when used as a unary operator). ;)
(; //LEAN:Neg.neg ;)
Neg__neg : (x : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: The denominator of the rational number is a natural number. ;)
(; //LEAN:Rat.den ;)
Rat__den : (self : Elem Rat) -> Elem Nat.

(; doc: The denominator is nonzero. ;)
(; //LEAN:Rat.den_nz ;)
Rat__den_nz : (self : Elem Rat) -> Proof (Neq (Rat__den self) 0).

(; //LEAN:Rat.den_pos ;)
Rat__den_pos : (self : Elem Rat) -> Proof (Lt 0 (Rat__den self)).

(; doc: Form the quotient `n / d` where `n d : Int`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Rat.divInt ;)
Rat__divInt : (x : Elem Int) -> (x1 : Elem Int) -> Elem Rat.

(; //LEAN:Rat.divInt_neg' ;)
Rat__divInt_neg' : (num : Elem Int) -> (den : Elem Int) -> Proof (Eq (Rat__divInt num (Neg__neg den)) (Rat__divInt (Neg__neg num) den)).

(; //LEAN:Rat.divInt_zero ;)
Rat__divInt_zero : (n : Elem Int) -> Proof (Eq (Rat__divInt n 0) 0).

(; //LEAN:Rat.instIntCast ;)
Rat__instIntCast : Proof (IntCast Rat).

(; //LEAN:Rat.instNatCast ;)
Rat__instNatCast : Proof (NatCast Rat).

(; //LEAN:Rat.intCast_inj ;)
Rat__intCast_inj : (a : Elem Int) -> (b : Elem Int) -> Proof (iff (Eq a b) (Eq a b)).

(; doc: Is this rational number integral? ;)
(; //LEAN:Rat.isInt ;)
Rat__isInt : (a : Elem Rat) -> Elem Bool.

(; doc: Constructs a rational number from components. We rename the constructor to `mk'` to avoid a clash with the smart constructor. ;)
(; //LEAN:Rat.mk' ;)
Rat__mk' : (num : Elem Int) -> (den : Elem Nat) -> (den_nz : Proof (autoParam (Neq den 0) Rat__den_nz___autoParam)) -> (reduced : Proof (autoParam (Nat__Coprime (Int__natAbs num) den) Rat__reduced___autoParam)) -> Elem Rat.

(; //LEAN:Rat.mk'_mul_mk'._proof_1 ;)
Rat__mk'_mul_mk'___proof_1 : (x : Elem Int) -> (x1 : Elem Int) -> (x2 : Elem Nat) -> (x3 : Elem Nat) -> (h : Proof (Nat__Coprime (Int__natAbs x) x2)) -> (h1 : Proof (Nat__Coprime (Int__natAbs x1) x3)) -> (h2 : Proof (Nat__Coprime (Int__natAbs x) x3)) -> (h3 : Proof (Nat__Coprime (Int__natAbs x1) x2)) -> Proof (Nat__Coprime (Int__natAbs (times x x1)) (times x2 x3)).

(; //LEAN:Rat.mk'_pow._proof_2 ;)
Rat__mk'_pow___proof_2 : (den : Elem Nat) -> (hd : Proof (Neq den 0)) -> (n : Elem Nat) -> Proof (Neq (pow den n) 0).

(; //LEAN:Rat.mk'_pow._proof_3 ;)
Rat__mk'_pow___proof_3 : (num : Elem Int) -> (den : Elem Nat) -> (hdn : Proof (Nat__Coprime (Int__natAbs num) den)) -> (n : Elem Nat) -> Proof (Nat__Coprime (Int__natAbs (pow num n)) (pow den n)).

(; //LEAN:Rat.mkRat_self ;)
Rat__mkRat_self : (a : Elem Rat) -> Proof (Eq (mkRat (Rat__num a) (Rat__den a)) a).

(; //LEAN:Rat.mk_eq_divInt ;)
Rat__mk_eq_divInt : (num : Elem Int) -> (den : Elem Nat) -> (nz : Proof (Neq den 0)) -> (c : Proof (Nat__Coprime (Int__natAbs num) den)) -> Proof (Eq (Rat__mk' num den nz c) (Rat__divInt num den)).

(; //LEAN:Rat.natCast_inj ;)
Rat__natCast_inj : (a : Elem Nat) -> (b : Elem Nat) -> Proof (iff (Eq a b) (Eq a b)).

(; doc: The numerator of the rational number is an integer. ;)
(; //LEAN:Rat.num ;)
Rat__num : (self : Elem Rat) -> Elem Int.

(; //LEAN:Rat.num_eq_zero ;)
Rat__num_eq_zero : (q : Elem Rat) -> Proof (iff (Eq (Rat__num q) 0) (Eq q 0)).

(; doc: Embedding of `Int` in the rational numbers. ;)
(; //LEAN:Rat.ofInt ;)
Rat__ofInt : (num : Elem Int) -> Elem Rat.

(; //LEAN:Rat.ofInt._proof_1 ;)
Rat__ofInt___proof_1 : (num : Elem Int) -> Proof (Nat__Coprime (Int__natAbs num) 1).

(; doc: The numerator and denominator are coprime: it is in "reduced form". ;)
(; //LEAN:Rat.reduced ;)
Rat__reduced : (self : Elem Rat) -> Proof (Nat__Coprime (Int__natAbs (Rat__num self)) (Rat__den self)).

(; //LEAN:Rat.zero_divInt ;)
Rat__zero_divInt : (n : Elem Int) -> Proof (Eq (Rat__divInt 0 n) 0).

(; doc: `True` is a proposition and has only an introduction rule, `True.intro : True`. In other words, `True` is simply true, and has a canonical proof, `True.intro` For more information: [Propositional Logic](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/propositions_and_proofs.html#propositional-logic) ;)
(; //LEAN:True ;)
True : Prop.

(; doc: Congruence in the function argument: if `a₁ = a₂` then `f a₁ = f a₂` for any (nondependent) function `f`. This is more powerful than it might look at first, because you can also use a lambda expression for `f` to prove that `<something containing a₁> = <something containing a₂>`. This function is used internally by tactics like `congr` and `simp` to apply equalities inside subterms.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:congrArg ;)
congrArg : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X x x1)) -> Proof (Eqt Y (f x) (f x1)).

(; doc: Similar to `congrFun` but `β` does not depend on `α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:congrFun' ;)
congrFun' : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (g : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt (Elem X -> Y) f g)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt Y (f a) (g a)).

(; doc: **Alias** of `congrArg`. ;)
(; //LEAN:congr_arg ;)
congr_arg : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X x x1)) -> Proof (Eqt Y (f x) (f x1)).

(; //LEAN:eq_self ;)
eq_self : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt Prop (Eqt X a a) True).

(; //LEAN:instInhabitedRat._proof_1 ;)
instInhabitedRat___proof_1 : Proof (Neq 1 0).

(; //LEAN:instNatCastInt ;)
instNatCastInt : Proof (NatCast Int).

(; doc: Construct a rational number from a numerator and denominator. This is a "smart constructor" that divides the numerator and denominator by the gcd to ensure that the resulting rational number is normalized, and returns zero if `den` is zero. ;)
(; //LEAN:mkRat ;)
mkRat : (num : Elem Int) -> (den : Elem Nat) -> Elem Rat.

(; //LEAN:of_eq_true ;)
of_eq_true : (p : Prop) -> (h : Proof (Eqt Prop p True)) -> Proof p.

(; doc: `rfl : a = a` is the unique constructor of the equality type. This is the same as `Eq.refl` except that it takes `a` implicitly instead of explicitly.  This is a more powerful theorem than it may appear at first, because although the statement of the theorem is `a = a`, Lean will allow anything that is definitionally equal to that type. So, for instance, `2 + 2 = 4` is proven in Lean by `rfl`, because both sides are the same up to definitional equality. ;)
(; //LEAN:rfl ;)
rfl : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X a a).

